    "bounding box" : [ 327.959, 685.706, 370.657, 697.673 ],
    "font" : "CMMI12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "M(k,g)."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 9,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 56.693, 633.781, 538.588, 670.966 ],
    "font" : "SFRM1200",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Proof. Note that b2(k,g,s) ≥ 12M(k,g). It is easy to check that the statement is true for k = 3 and g ≤ 8 (see Table 1), for other values we can use Lemma 7 and it is sufficient to prove"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 11,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 56.693, 569.203, 538.584, 642.075 ],
    "font" : "SFRM1200",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "|G| ≥ b2(k,g,(k − 2)g) which is equivalent to |G| > b1(k,g,(k − 2)g). This is clearly true for s = 0. We will proceed by induction as follows. Let G be a nontrivial (k,g,s)-multipole of order n with 0 < s ≤ (k−2)g and assume that Theorem 9 holds for all nontrivial (k′,g′,s′)-multipoles of order n′ with s′ ≤ (k′ − 2)g′ where (k′,g′,s′,n′) is lexicographically smaller than (k,g,s,n). By Lemma 3 we also assume that G contains no vertex with inner degree 1."
  }, {
    "type" : "heading",
    "pdfua_tag" : "H1",
    "id" : 12,
    "level" : "Doctitle",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 56.693, 548.828, 348.284, 563.665 ],
    "heading level" : 1,
    "font" : "SFBX1200",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Case 1: G contains a vertex v with inner degree 2"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 13,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 56.693, 468.9, 538.586, 549.422 ],
    "font" : "SFRM1200",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "If k ≥ 4, then let G′ = G − v. Clearly, G′ is a (k,g,s − (k − 4))-multipole of order n − 1. Graph G′ cannot be trivial, otherwise either G would be trivial, or it would have girth less than g (≥ 5). But then G′ fulfils the theorem condition and as (k,g,s − (k − 4),n − 1) is lexicographically smaller than (k,g,s,n) we have |G′| ≥ b2(k,g,s − (k − 4)). By Lemma 7, |G′| ≥ b2(k,g,(k − 2)g)) and then |G| ≥ b2(k,g,(k − 2)g) + 1. Thus, k = 3."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 14,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 56.693, 396.671, 538.588, 477.193 ],
    "font" : "SFRM1200",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "For k = 3 we already resolved the cases when g ≤ 8. Thus, we may assume g ≥ 9. We remove the vertex v from G and join the two links formerly incident with v to produce one new link. The constructed multipole G′ is clearly a nontrivial (3,g − 1,s − 1)-multipole with s − 1 ≤ (k − 2)(g − 1). By the induction hypothesis and Lemma 7 we have |G| ≥ b2(3,g − 1,s − 1) ≥ b2(3,g − 1,g − 1). So it is sufficient to show that"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 15,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 222.076, 370.27, 373.2, 391.012 ],
    "font" : "CMMI12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "b2(3,g − 1,g − 1) > b1(3,g,g)"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 16,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 56.693, 337.074, 538.587, 366.608 ],
    "font" : "SFRM1200",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "We manually checked that this is true for all g < 14. For g ≥ 14, by Proposition 8 it is sufficient to show"
  }, {
